[{:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-93735"] :question/problem "Given that \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\n express the quadratic form $Q(x)={x}_{1}^{2}+3{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}$ in terms of the components of $y.$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\ngives the following expressions for ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & ={x}_{1}+2{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{2} & =-{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy solving the second equation for ${x}_{2}$ and substituting it into the first equation, we obtain the following expressions for ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={y}_{1}+2{y}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2} & =-{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute these into our quadratic form expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & ={x}_{1}^{2}+3{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =({y}_{1}+2{y}_{2})^{2}+3({y}_{1}+2{y}_{2})(-{y}_{2})+(-{y}_{2})^{2} \\\\  & ={y}_{1}^{2}+4{y}_{1}{y}_{2}+4{y}_{2}^{2}-3{y}_{1}{y}_{2}-6{y}_{2}^{2}+{y}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & ={y}_{1}^{2}+{y}_{1}{y}_{2}-{y}_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-94180"] :question/problem "Given that \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\n express the quadratic form $Q(x)={x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{1}{x}_{2}-2{x}_{2}^{2}$ in terms of the components of $y.$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\ngives the following expressions for ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & ={x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{2} & =-{x}_{1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving these for ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =-{y}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2} & ={y}_{1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute these into our quadratic form expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & ={x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{1}{x}_{2}-2{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =(-{y}_{2})^{2}+(-{y}_{2}){y}_{1}-2{y}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & ={y}_{2}^{2}-{y}_{2}{y}_{1}-2{y}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =-2{y}_{1}^{2}-{y}_{1}{y}_{2}+{y}_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-94182/selection/82d3b0180362bb5ee1b7f6635b8f2ce2a950efb8d7d59042e11cbf2f3cf02270" :answer/id #uuid "6389fd0c-d3b8-5052-8107-67733391b421" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}21 & 26 \\\\ 26 & 32\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-94182/selection/559043e1adcb7864dce161830f30917a5b2d863ce80268b9adf7a3de71165c0e" :answer/id #uuid "2909b54e-42ae-5da0-b933-44328dd00ba3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}11 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 12\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-94182/selection/2854765a7eeaa360b540a6317bd15011f3f668688ffecf67c8cf24d0037a5b06" :answer/id #uuid "defd1f32-35bd-5421-b361-7cf2b9c1b71b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}10 & 16 \\\\ 16 & 12\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-94182/selection/c139bbf7ebc327165bdae2b6f9914dd8c8af4236e83abe1b638c5346506ae99c" :answer/id #uuid "f53a6c78-1e79-50fa-a49f-589cd37609fe" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}12 & 15 \\\\ 15 & 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-94182/selection/0acba2537a99b8d7ea9f100d3e4b811af7f707f04f4f39a79efbc86d928e92f7" :answer/id #uuid "c24f5bda-215d-5ccf-9cec-040154c96e54" :answer/type :answer.type/math